      "number": 1,
      "title": "प्रथम अध्याय",
      "verses": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "sanskrit": "अथातो भक्तिजिज्ञासा ॥ १ ॥",
          "transliteration": "athāto bhaktijijñāsā || 1 ||",
          "translation": "Now, therefore, the inquiry into bhakti.",
          "hindi": "अब अतः भक्ति की जिज्ञासा।",
          "commentary": "Shandilya opens with almost the same formula as Narada — \"athāto\" — and almost the same as the Brahma Sutras (\"athāto brahma-jijñāsā\"). The parallel is deliberate: Shandilya places the inquiry into bhakti at the same philosophical level as the inquiry into Brahman. Just as the Brahma Sutras ask \"what is Brahman?\" Shandilya asks \"what is bhakti?\" — treating it not as a subordinate practice but as an independent object of philosophical investigation deserving its own systematic treatment. The word \"jijñāsā\" (inquiry, the desire to know) frames this as an intellectual and spiritual quest together: you cannot understand bhakti from outside; you must become a bhakta-jijñāsu — one who both practises and seeks to understand.",
          "wordMeaning": "Shandilya opens with almost the same formula as Narada — \"athāto\" — and almost the same as the Brahma Sutras (\"athāto brahma-jijñāsā\"). The parallel is deliberate: Shandilya places the inquiry into bhakti at the same philosophical level as the inquiry into Brahman. Just as the Brahma Sutras ask \"what is Brahman?\" Shandilya asks \"what is bhakti?\" — treating it not as a subordinate practice but as an independent object of philosophical investigation deserving its own systematic treatment. The word \"jijñāsā\" (inquiry, the desire to know) frames this as an intellectual and spiritual quest together: you cannot understand bhakti from outside; you must become a bhakta-jijñāsu — one who both practises and seeks to understand.",
          "science": "अब अतः भक्ति की जिज्ञासा। श्रद्धा और सुरक्षित लगाव तनाव-प्रतिक्रिया को घटाते हैं। यह पंक्ति उसी आश्रय को अपना विषय बनाती है।"
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "sanskrit": "सा परानुरक्तिरीश्वरे ॥ २ ॥",
          "transliteration": "sā parānuraktirīśvare || 2 ||",
          "translation": "That (bhakti) is supreme attachment (para-anurakti) to Ishvara.",
          "hindi": "वह (भक्ति) ईश्वर में परानुरक्ति (परम आसक्ति) है।",
          "commentary": "Shandilya's definition in four words. \"Para-anurakti\" — supreme attachment. \"Anurakti\" differs from \"prema\" (Narada's term): anurakti carries the connotation of sustained, deepening attachment that grows over time — the kind of love that binds a devoted disciple to a teacher, a child to a parent, a true devotee to their chosen form of the divine. \"Para\" (supreme) distinguishes this from ordinary worldly attachment (which Shandilya will discuss as an obstacle to bhakti). And the direction: \"Ishvare\" — in or toward Ishvara. Not a diffuse spirituality but a specific, directed attachment to the divine reality. The comparison with Narada: Narada says bhakti IS love; Shandilya says bhakti is ATTACHMENT — the ongoing quality of being drawn toward and held by the divine.",
          "wordMeaning": "Shandilya's definition in four words. \"Para-anurakti\" — supreme attachment. \"Anurakti\" differs from \"prema\" (Narada's term): anurakti carries the connotation of sustained, deepening attachment that grows over time — the kind of love that binds a devoted disciple to a teacher, a child to a parent, a true devotee to their chosen form of the divine. \"Para\" (supreme) distinguishes this from ordinary worldly attachment (which Shandilya will discuss as an obstacle to bhakti). And the direction: \"Ishvare\" — in or toward Ishvara. Not a diffuse spirituality but a specific, directed attachment to the divine reality. The comparison with Narada: Narada says bhakti IS love; Shandilya says bhakti is ATTACHMENT — the ongoing quality of being drawn toward and held by the divine.",
          "science": "वह (भक्ति) ईश्वर में परानुरक्ति (परम आसक्ति) है। श्रद्धा और सुरक्षित लगाव तनाव-प्रतिक्रिया को घटाते हैं। यह पंक्ति उसी आश्रय को अपना विषय बनाती है।"
        },
